 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "The kingdom of Kongo lay south of the Congo River mouth (today's Angola, DR Congo and Republic of Congo).\nFounding: about the late 14th century by Lukeni lua Nimi. The king was the Manikongo, with his capital at Mbanza Kongo. Provinces were ruled by governors who paid tribute.\nEconomy: farming, weaving raffia cloth, iron and copper working, trade; nzimbu shells were used as money.\nContact with Portugal: Diogo Cão arrived in 1483. King Nzinga a Nkuwu was baptised as João I in 1491. His son Afonso I (Nzinga Mbemba, ruled 1506–1543) became a Christian, built churches and schools, and wrote letters to the king of Portugal complaining about the slave trade.\nDecline: the slave trade weakened the kingdom; wars and quarrels over succession; the Portuguese defeated Kongo at the battle of Mbwila in 1665.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "a": 1483,
   "b": 1665
  },
  "problem": "How long after the arrival of Diogo Cão ({a}) was the battle of Mbwila ({b})?",
  "steps": [
   "Both years are AD, so subtract.",
   "{b} − {a} = {b - a} years."
  ],
  "answer": "{b - a} years",
  "checks": []
 },
